WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.530
- What was the pain like during the fire?

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.443
- I had none.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.323
- None?

00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:07.156
- None.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.600
- [Narrator] If you were alone in a room

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.900
with a burn survivor, what would you ask?

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.480
- Do you think pretty privilege exists?

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.430
- After the accident
did you have a hard time

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.498
looking at yourself in the mirror?

00:00:19.498 --> 00:00:22.998
(haunting keyboard music)

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.290
- Hello.
- Hi.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.610
- How are you?

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.170
- I'm good. How are you?

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.490
- Doing great. Doing great.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.510
- And what's your name?

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:34.680
- Jay.
- Jay.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.230
It's lovely to meet you, Jay.
- Same here.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.570
- Do you mind talking about your accident?

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.470
- Sure.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.850
I lived in Puerto Rico.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.310
It was in 1998 after Hurricane George

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.520
and we didn't have electricity
for more than a month

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.680
and I had a couple of seizures

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.580
and I went through several
neurological studies.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.310
I had glue on my scalp

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.270
I had to remove with some
type of oily substance.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.240
My dad was outside dealing
with the generator,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.270
so what I did was grab a candle,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.690
just started pouring
the grease over my hair.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.760
And when I lean forward

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.950
to see myself better on this area,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.150
on the left side,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.610
that's where my hair actually slipped

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.940
over my shoulder and touch
the flame, the candle.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.640
And in less than a second,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.553
I was just a human torch.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.670
- That must have been really difficult.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.520
- What was the pain like during the fire?

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.513
- I had none.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:35.453
- None?

00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:36.286
- None.

00:01:36.286 --> 00:01:38.013
My burns, they were,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.680
deep, third degree,

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:41.880
so,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.420
most of my nerves were damaged.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.810
What I felt was extreme heat.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.690
I remember swallowing the flames

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.500
and feeling it all the way in my lungs,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.190
but in that moment I had no pain at all.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.150
- Wow.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.820
So, it just completely
nothing, to you at all.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.200
- Completely nothing, no.
- Wow.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.290
- I have never experienced

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.020
that kind of peace ever.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.160
- Wow.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.480
That's insane.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.770
- Yes. (chuckling)

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.630
- Right, what was your
hospital experience like

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.653
after being burned?

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.520
- That's a very intense question.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.353
Even my,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.000
my heart is racing a little.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.830
That experience was,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.850
very, very intense,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.780
because I was in a induced coma.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.970
But still, you as a patient,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.130
you can actually hear,

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.617
and I remember the doctors
betting on my life,

00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.740
"I don't think she's gonna be,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.857
more than 12 hours."

00:02:41.857 --> 00:02:44.160
"I don't give her more than 16 hours."

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.320
And I'm here having this experience,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.660
I'm trying to scream my lungs out,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.630
like, "No, no, no, no,
no, I have more time."

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.930
And then I started recovering,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.400
and debridement,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.620
I don't know if you know what that means.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.940
- I don't.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:00.773
- You don't?

00:03:00.773 --> 00:03:03.450
So, that's the process
that us burn survivors

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.940
have to go through and
is where they actually

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.790
have to scrape the dead
skin from your body.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.060
And even though, yes, they put sedation,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.540
my body got used to the
medication so quickly,

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.290
that I actually felt every second of it.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.850
I remember,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.200
having to,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.000
go through a lot of deep breaths

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.700
and hold my tears

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.560
and try not to move as much,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.530
so they could get through
it as quick as possible.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.363
But yeah,

00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:34.320
it's been,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.780
kind of bittersweet. (chuckling)

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.600
- Well, I'm glad that you're still here.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.160
- Yes, thank you.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.600
- About the accident.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.450
What triggers you and
how do you cope with it?

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.540
- When I'm close to a
fireplace or a bonfire

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.450
where I feel like extreme heat,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.283
I feel it,

00:03:52.283 --> 00:03:54.420
and I get that feeling,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.140
of the moment where I was
actually swallowing the flames.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.930
But what I do is I just breathe

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.350
and even though I've been
through so much in life,

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:05.340
I'm still here.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.480
I have a second chance

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.120
and I'm trying to make the best out of it.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.017
- Do you feel that your quote unquote

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.440
"normal day to day life" has changed,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.000
and how so?

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.690
- I do have certain physical limitations

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.640
and my skin, it's so tight,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.690
specially around my
neck that my skin pulls.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.690
It's very hard for me even to sleep.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.290
I get spasms pretty much every single day

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.850
and I get migraines

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.850
and my hands, sometimes
they don't work completely.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.470
And that could be a
little bit frustrating,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.360
because I wanna do so much.
- Mm hm.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.790
- Have people treated you differently

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.370
or even looked at you
differently in any aspect?

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.980
- Do you ever feel alienated
in your day to day life?

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:50.813
Like do people,

00:04:50.813 --> 00:04:52.680
do you feel people looking at you weird?

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.630
- Oh yes, definitely.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:59.630
I've had people that ask me
if I wanted to kill myself,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.310
if I ever contemplated suicide,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:07.080
if I feel ashamed for the
way that I look, my skin.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.310
If I go to a restaurant,

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.340
I've had situations where I had someone

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.180
across the restaurant just
completely staring at me

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.820
and I'm here like, "Yes, I have a mouth.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.920
I eat just like you do.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.110
I drink just like you do.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.360
I walk just like you do."

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.570
But yes, society has been very cruel.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.120
People have spat on my face,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:30.953
pulled my hair,

00:05:30.953 --> 00:05:34.927
just trying to see if
I have a wig on or not.

00:05:34.927 --> 00:05:38.700
I've been called, Scarfaced, you name it.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.570
- Wow.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.240
- Do you think pretty privilege exists?

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.830
- Yes.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.230
I have actually witnessed that.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.470
So as a teenager, I wanted to apply

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.417
for a job at a store and they said,

00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:54.330
"No, we're not hiring."

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.830
And two more girls come in
asking for an application

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.930
and they got the fricking application.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.420
And I'm here like, "Oh, wow.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:04.253
So,

00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:06.450
just because I'm burned,

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:10.440
means that I'm not good enough to work?"

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.360
So, unfortunately, yeah,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.660
that happens quite often.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.060
- How have your experiences
affected your relationship

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.890
with those around you?

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.720
- Well, I,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:22.800
have come to learn

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.740
that people show their true colors

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.320
when something like this,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.790
changed your entire life.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.250
And I did have one situation

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.827
where a family member
asked me over the phone,

00:06:35.827 --> 00:06:37.920
"Whenever you're done with your surgeries

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.030
I would like to see you,

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.390
to see if you are gonna
be a valuable person

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.850
in my family and in society."

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.200
That was very hurtful.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.510
I did let it get to me

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.490
and I went through therapy

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.490
for insomnia and depression for a while.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.730
- Do you still feel a little
resentment toward that person?

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:00.413
- That person passed away years ago.

00:07:00.413 --> 00:07:03.810
When I saw him laying down in that casket,

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.440
I felt very sad for him.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.180
And I,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.710
actually,

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.150
forgave him,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.620
for his ignorance,

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:18.000
and I released myself from that burden.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.190
- That takes a lot to forgive somebody

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.660
for doing something like that.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.070
- Did you feel nervous at all

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.563
about being asked these questions?

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.570
- I did.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.910
Before I came here yesterday,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.690
I remember sitting in
a corner at the airport

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.960
in Puerto Rico and I started to cry.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.870
The fact that I have the opportunity

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.270
to bring awareness,

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.140
is very special to me,

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.420
because it needs to be done.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:49.710
It needs to be done.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.507
Like we say in Spanish,

00:07:51.507 --> 00:07:52.897
(speaking foreign language)

00:07:52.897 --> 00:07:54.870
"I have a thick skin,"

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:56.940
but not everyone does.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.150
And to me it's very, very special.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.780
So I'm very, very grateful
for every single person

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.720
that have been seated
next to me. (chuckling)

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.280
- Describe your best and
worst day since your accident.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.990
- My,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:14.580
worst day,

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.310
was when I had my surgery

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.700
from the tissue expander.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.000
They had to put a skin graft
on the center of my head.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.830
The pain was,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.360
insane.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.000
I think that was the
the worst that I've had.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.920
My best day,

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.330
I have two,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.700
and is the moment I met both

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.470
of my daughters when they were born.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:42.060
They have pushed me to be a better woman,

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:43.620
a better human being,

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.180
a better person.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.900
And everything that I
do, I do it for them.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.450
- So you have children?
- Yes, I do.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.283
- That's awesome.

00:08:52.283 --> 00:08:55.050
What do you feel that you can teach them,

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:58.320
that if you hadn't had those experiences,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.510
you think you might not
have otherwise been able to?

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.120
- Something that I teach
them every single day

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.600
is to accept themselves as they are.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.360
That they should love themselves,

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.620
inside out.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.770
That love should translate

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.060
through our smile,

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.800
through our eyes,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.720
through the way that we carry ourselves,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.200
through the way that we actually
interact with other people.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.367
And that's something that I
always teach 'em, you know,

00:09:24.367 --> 00:09:27.420
"You need to try to keep your heart

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.750
as pure and as loving as possible,

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.330
because that's what's gonna take you,

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.100
moving forward in your life.":

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.840
- Well, this is my last question,

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.360
but can I give you a hug?

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.610
- Yes, definitely.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.370
- Hi.
- Hi. (chuckling)

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.203
- Thank you.

00:09:46.818 --> 00:09:49.920
Aw, you gonna make me cry. (laughing)

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.153
- I'm already crying. (sobbing)

00:09:54.184 --> 00:09:55.033
- Oh, thank you, thank you, thank you,

00:09:55.033 --> 00:09:56.160
thank you, thank you.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:57.753
God bless you.
- You too.

00:09:58.714 --> 00:10:01.447
- Hmm, I'm gonna take you home.

00:10:01.447 --> 00:10:03.697
(laughing)

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:06.390
- Ah.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:07.754
- Well, that's-
- There you go.

00:10:07.754 --> 00:10:09.220
Oh, no, no, no, don't cry.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:10.977
(laughing)

00:10:10.977 --> 00:10:13.851
- I'm sorry.(laughing)

